⭐ Memory Notes:
->Why do we use Pair?
A TreeNode only stores:
- value
- left child
- right child

But this algorithm also needs to remember
which traversal is currently being performed.

So we create:
Pair = (TreeNode, State)

->Why new Pair(root,1)?
Create a new object that stores:

Node = root
State = 1 (Preorder)
Then push that single object into the stack.

->Why st.push(it) again?
Because the same node still has work left.

State 1 -> Preorder completed

Still need:

State 2 -> Inorder

State 3 -> Postorder

So push it back after increasing its state.

->Why it.num++?
1 -> 2

Meaning:

Next time perform Inorder.


2 -> 3

Meaning:

Next time perform Postorder.

->Why new Pair(it.node.left,1)?
Whenever we move to a new child,

it always starts from State = 1.

Because no traversal has been done
for that child yet.

⭐ One-Line Memory Trick
State 1 → PRE → Push Back → Go Left
State 2 → IN → Push Back → Go Right
State 3 → POST → Finished
